---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayCategoryHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for EB-2 Backlog Applicants | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for EB-2 backlog applicants explains why some people with a pending or planned EB-2 petition evaluate EB-5 as a parallel or alternative path, and what to review with an attorney first.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb2-to-eb5`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for EB-2 Backlog Applicants';
const subhead =
  'What it means to evaluate EB-5 when an EB-2 petition is pending, planned, or stuck behind country-of-birth demand.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Immigrant Investor Program: Complete Guide',
    url: '/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide',
    description: 'Program structure, reserved categories, and filing overview.',
  },
  {
    title: 'H-1B to Green Card via EB-5',
    url: '/pathways/h1b-to-green-card',
    description: 'Employer-independence and concurrent filing framing for H-1B households.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayCategoryHero('status-transition');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway for transitioning from EB-2 to EB-5"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 Alongside an EB-2 Case"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for EB-2 Backlog"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for EB-2 backlog',
    'EB-2 to EB-5',
    'employment-based backlog EB-5',
    'EB-2 parallel EB-5',
  ]}
>
  <h2>What an EB-2 case usually represents</h2>
  <p>
    EB-2 is an employment-based immigrant category generally used by professionals with advanced degrees or exceptional
    ability, often tied to an employer-sponsored petition (and, in many cases, a labor certification process). Having a
    pending or planned EB-2 case means you are already on an employment-based track toward permanent residence, but
    visa number availability still depends on country of birth and overall demand. When those numbers are backlogged,
    families can wait years after I-140 approval before a green card becomes available. Current category dates belong on
    the
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    >, not in static estimates on this page.
  </p>

  <h2>Why some EB-2 applicants look at EB-5</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5 is a separate immigrant category built around a qualifying investment and job-creation requirements, not around
    employer sponsorship. Some applicants evaluate it as a parallel or alternative path when EB-2 visa availability
    feels open-ended, when employer dependence is a concern, or when a spouse and children need a timeline that is not
    controlled by a single employer's petition strategy. EB-5 still has its own capital, documentation, and category
    rules. It is not a guaranteed faster outcome. Program overview:
    <a href="/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide">complete EB-5 guide</a>. Shared concurrent-filing framing for many
    nonimmigrants already in the U.S. is on
    <a href="/pathways/h1b-to-green-card">H-1B to green card via EB-5</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Pursuing EB-5 without automatically abandoning EB-2</h2>
  <p>
    Filing an EB-5 petition does not, by itself, require you to abandon an existing EB-2 case. Whether maintaining both
    tracks makes sense, and how priority dates, adjustment eligibility, and immigrant visa processing interact, is
    case-specific. Some households keep an employment-based petition active while assembling an EB-5 filing; others
    switch focus entirely. Do not assume either approach is correct for your facts. Review strategy with an immigration
    attorney before filing EB-5 alongside, or instead of, an EB-2 matter.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
